ROAST.PET FRONTIER STACK =E2=80=94 1 USEFUL FIND

DaVinci Resolve Studio 21.1 native MCP server =E2=80=94 TEST

Source: https://www.blackmagicdesign.com/media/release/20260908-03
Release: 2026-09-08 (newly discovered by scanner, not released this hour)

What changed: Resolve Studio 21.1 adds a native MCP server for
external AI assistants plus expanded scripting/automation. Assistants
can analyze projects, organize media, change settings, create edits,
remove clips, batch render and control production tasks. 21.1 also
expands scripting access to timeline Inspector properties,
transitions, fades, speed changes, audio normalization and rendering
controls.

Roast.pet relevance: this can become the deterministic finishing layer
after generative video. Instead of asking video foundation models to
solve captions, exact timing, audio leveling, QR end cards,
aspect-ratio variants and delivery renders, generate the pet
performance upstream and hand the result to a programmable
professional NLE.

Inputs/outputs: generated video/audio/images + edit instructions ->
Resolve timeline/project -> deterministic mastered MP4 variants and
delivery assets.

API/install route: DaVinci Resolve Studio 21.1 native MCP server;
Python scripting is also available for deterministic automation.
Studio is required for native MCP/external automation. Linux support
is documented for Rocky Linux 8.6; macOS 15+ Apple Silicon and Windows
are also supported.

Pricing: Resolve Studio is paid desktop software; no per-render
inference charge. Verify current regional license price before
purchase.
Commercial license: commercial post-production use supported as normal
Resolve Studio software; generated-media licenses remain governed by
upstream model providers.
Privacy/self-hosting: excellent for the finishing stage. Media can
remain on the machine running Resolve rather than being uploaded to
another editing SaaS. MCP assistant traffic depends on whichever
assistant/model is connected.
Quality/latency constraints: deterministic editing/rendering rather
than generative inference. Requires a machine capable of Resolve; not
appropriate for the current low-power laptop, so this belongs on a
capable worker/GPU box. Project compatibility warning: projects
opened/created in 21.1 are not accessible in Resolve 20.3.

Exact Roast.pet use case:
canonical pet -> generated talking-pet clip -> Resolve MCP/Python ->
trim dead frames -> align selected TTS -> normalize audio ->
deterministic subtitles -> punchline zoom/SFX -> QR/end card -> render
Etsy preview + 9:16 social + AR asset.

Smallest prototype: take 10 existing roast clips and create one fixed
Resolve project template. Expose a single automation command that
swaps source media/text, applies subtitle and audio presets, adds the
QR/end card and batch-renders 1:1, 9:16 and AR variants. Compare
output consistency and human editing time against the current
FFmpeg/custom-script path.

Decision: TEST. Do not make it a hard dependency until the same
template is benchmarked against a lightweight FFmpeg/Remotion
pipeline.

TOP 5 STACK CHANGES TO TEST NOW
1. DaVinci Resolve 21.1 MCP/Python as deterministic finishing layer vs
custom FFmpeg/Remotion.
2. Wan 2.7 audio-driven first/last-frame I2V vs H3 Max Lip Sync.
3. Alphaveil/BiRefNet -> EdgeTAM -> ViTMatte local customer-photo preparati=
on.
4. Multi-reference canonical pet identity workflow.
5. Cheap video/TTS generation -> automated QC -> premium escalation.

CHEAPEST MEANINGFUL CAPABILITY IMPROVEMENT
Browser-local pet cutout/refinement remains effectively $0 marginal inferen=
ce.

BIGGEST CAPABILITY SHOCK THIS HOUR
Not a new foundation model: a major professional NLE now has an
official agent-control surface. That means Roast.pet can separate
stochastic generation from deterministic finishing instead of
accumulating fragile custom editing code.

CONCRETE STACK SIMPLIFICATION
Treat generation and finishing as separate contracts:
PetPerformanceRenderer -> raw performance clip
RoastFinisher -> exact captions/audio/SFX/QR/aspect ratios/delivery renders

Prototype Resolve 21.1 as RoastFinisher while keeping an
FFmpeg/Remotion implementation as the cheap headless fallback.

No other result cleared the novelty/relevance threshold; previously
captured H3 Max/Turbo/Lip Sync, Wan 2.6/2.7, Tsubaki.3, Alphaveil,
Qwen3-TTS and related items were suppressed rather than padded.
